1
00:00:00,249 --> 00:00:04,796
Your mom texts you: "Call me when you get a sec." No emoji,

2
00:00:04,796 --> 00:00:07,399
no "love you", just that.

3
00:00:07,399 --> 00:00:10,791
And your brain has already written the next five minutes of your life.

4
00:00:10,791 --> 00:00:13,925
Somebody's sick, or you forgot a birthday.

5
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:15,957
You've seen that exact text before,

6
00:00:15,957 --> 00:00:19,045
and your brain guessed what usually comes next.

7
00:00:19,045 --> 00:00:22,328
That's pretty much how ChatGPT works. Same text,

8
00:00:22,328 --> 00:00:28,214
but now she adds a party emoji. Nobody's sick. Somebody's engaged.

9
00:00:28,214 --> 00:00:31,782
One emoji flipped every guess you were about to make.

10
00:00:31,782 --> 00:00:35,044
That's the problem ChatGPT solves all day:

11
00:00:35,044 --> 00:00:39,285
given everything written so far, what probably comes next?

12
00:00:39,285 --> 00:00:43,834
And somehow, that same trick writes working code. Let's take it apart.

13
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:48,054
Most people assume it looks your question up somewhere. It doesn't.

14
00:00:48,054 --> 00:00:49,864
There's no answer sheet in there.

15
00:00:49,864 --> 00:00:52,764
It predicts the reply one small chunk at a time,

16
00:00:52,764 --> 00:00:56,097
sticks that chunk on the end, and predicts again.

17
00:00:56,097 --> 00:00:58,633
If you see it searching the web and showing links,

18
00:00:58,633 --> 00:01:02,876
that's an extra step bolted on top. Underneath, it's still this.

19
00:01:02,876 --> 00:01:05,676
The chunks are called tokens, bits of words,

20
00:01:05,676 --> 00:01:07,422
but "word" is close enough.

21
00:01:07,422 --> 00:01:10,664
A normal answer goes around that loop a few hundred times.

22
00:01:10,664 --> 00:01:13,788
When the text streams in on your screen, you're watching it happen.

23
00:01:14,407 --> 00:01:18,151
You've got a tiny version of this in your pocket. Pause the video,

24
00:01:18,151 --> 00:01:19,620
open any chat,

25
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:25,370
type "I'm going to" and keep tapping the middle suggestion.

26
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:26,743
Mine gave me:

27
00:01:26,743 --> 00:01:32,237
"I'm going to go get some lunch now so I'll call when I'm on the road I have a couple things I have..."

28
00:01:32,237 --> 00:01:35,404
and it just kept going. Tell me in the comments what yours said.

29
00:01:36,006 --> 00:01:38,934
When someone cracked open Apple's keyboard model,

30
00:01:38,934 --> 00:01:41,946
it had about 34 million settings.

31
00:01:41,946 --> 00:01:46,908
The ones behind ChatGPT are likely thousands of times bigger,

32
00:01:46,908 --> 00:01:52,417
and they learned from more writing than a person could read in thousands of lifetimes.

33
00:01:52,417 --> 00:01:55,405
So the guesses get really, really good.

34
00:01:55,405 --> 00:01:58,129
Good enough that it stops feeling like guessing.

35
00:01:58,761 --> 00:02:02,407
Which raises the obvious question. If it's only guessing,

36
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:06,471
how does it do real work? For me it started small.

37
00:02:06,471 --> 00:02:09,342
It would clean up the grammar in my messages.

38
00:02:09,342 --> 00:02:11,262
Then I started pasting in code,

39
00:02:11,262 --> 00:02:15,441
and it would spot the wrong variable or the broken data structure.

40
00:02:15,441 --> 00:02:21,587
Then it got big. In 2025, AI built on this same next-word engine,

41
00:02:21,587 --> 00:02:24,196
plus a lot of extra training to reason,

42
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:26,831
sat the International Math Olympiad,

43
00:02:26,831 --> 00:02:29,978
under the same time limits as the kids.

44
00:02:29,978 --> 00:02:35,494
Google's wrote full proofs in plain English and scored right at the gold-medal line,

45
00:02:35,494 --> 00:02:40,746
confirmed by the Olympiad's own graders. Plenty of kids still beat it,

46
00:02:40,746 --> 00:02:47,340
but come on. Autocomplete did that. So is it just autocomplete?

47
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:51,681
At the basic level, yes. It's just not the kind you think.

48
00:02:53,033 --> 00:02:58,658
Finish this one. "Someone ate my leftovers. Jay was the only one home,

49
00:02:58,658 --> 00:03:04,549
and Jay swears he hates pasta. So it was..." It was Jay.

50
00:03:04,549 --> 00:03:06,321
It's always Jay.

51
00:03:06,321 --> 00:03:10,351
To get there you had to follow the clues and catch the lie.

52
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:13,761
Nobody wrote detective rules into ChatGPT.

53
00:03:13,761 --> 00:03:17,562
Getting really good at the next word forced it to pick that up.

54
00:03:17,562 --> 00:03:22,215
Code is the same game: keep track of what every variable is holding,

55
00:03:22,215 --> 00:03:23,529
the way you kept track of Jay.

56
00:03:24,172 --> 00:03:25,882
In 2025,

57
00:03:25,882 --> 00:03:33,118
researchers at Anthropic looked inside one of these models while it wrote a rhyming poem.

58
00:03:33,118 --> 00:03:35,200
Before it wrote the second line,

59
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,691
it had already picked the word it wanted to land on, "rabbit",

60
00:03:39,691 --> 00:03:44,596
and built the line to get there. So it writes one piece at a time,

61
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:49,202
but inside, it can be planning ahead. Geoffrey Hinton,

62
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:52,909
one of the godfathers of AI, put it this way:

63
00:03:52,909 --> 00:03:57,325
"to predict the next word, you have to understand the sentences."

64
00:03:57,325 --> 00:04:00,349
Others, like the linguist Emily Bender,

65
00:04:00,349 --> 00:04:03,626
say it's closer to a very fancy parrot.

66
00:04:03,626 --> 00:04:06,467
Smart people are still fighting about this one,

67
00:04:06,467 --> 00:04:08,213
so pick your side in the comments.

68
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:13,701
One more piece. Straight out of training, a model doesn't answer you.

69
00:04:13,701 --> 00:04:17,552
It just keeps writing. Ask "what's a good dinner tonight?"

70
00:04:17,552 --> 00:04:20,853
and it might carry on like a forum post:

71
00:04:20,853 --> 00:04:25,975
"...asking for my picky five-year-old. Edit: thanks everyone!"

72
00:04:25,975 --> 00:04:28,042
So the companies train it again,

73
00:04:28,042 --> 00:04:31,154
on good answers that real people wrote and rated,

74
00:04:31,154 --> 00:04:34,666
until it acts like an assistant instead of a comment section.

75
00:04:35,286 --> 00:04:37,641
Which leaves one catch.

76
00:04:37,641 --> 00:04:40,438
It's predicting what a good answer sounds like,

77
00:04:40,438 --> 00:04:44,920
and most of the time that's also what's true. But in 2025,

78
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:50,510
two real newspapers printed a summer reading list with a novel called Tidewater Dreams,

79
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:54,991
by Isabel Allende. It sounds exactly like a book she'd write.

80
00:04:54,991 --> 00:04:59,022
It doesn't exist. So here's the whole video in one line.

81
00:04:59,022 --> 00:05:03,098
It doesn't look things up. It guesses the next word, really,

82
00:05:03,098 --> 00:05:08,259
really well. Next video: why it makes things up with a straight face,

83
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:12,783
and the lawyers, judges and one airline that trusted it anyway.

84
00:05:12,783 --> 00:05:13,583
I gotchu.

